{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"No. Exchange volume shows recent trading in the ETF shares, but it is not a ceiling on what can trade: an ETF’s liquidity also depends on the liquidity of its underlying holdings and the market makers’ ability to hedge.\n\nAuthorized participants (APs) can create shares by delivering a basket of securities (or cash) to the fund in exchange for new ETF shares, and redeem ETF shares for the basket (or cash). This arbitrage mechanism helps align the ETF’s price with its net asset value, but it does not guarantee liquidity or a particular execution.\n\nAlso examine the bid–ask spread, quote depth and size, premiums or discounts to NAV or intraday indicative value, underlying-security liquidity and trading conditions, and—where available—creation/redemption basket details and recent flows. Check conditions at the time of the order; these indicators inform assessment, not assurance of execution capacity."}}
